        fp: "Defendant was walking through a dimly lit municipal parking lot late at night when he was approached by an aggressive panhandler who was holding a heavy metallic tire iron down at his side. The panhandler demanded twenty dollars. Because the parking structure had recently been the site of violent armed robberies, Defendant subjectively and genuinely panicked, believing the panhandler was about to strike him over the head with the iron. Defendant immediately pulled a licensed snub-nosed revolver from his jacket pocket and fired a single shot into the panhandler's abdomen, inflicting a fatal wound. At trial, the jury found that while Defendant genuinely and subjectively believed deadly physical force was necessary to protect his life, his belief was completely unreasonable under the objective circumstances because the panhandler had made no verbal threats of violence and had not raised the tire iron. The jurisdiction defines murder and manslaughter at common law and adheres to the doctrine of imperfect self-defense.",
